"""Shot-by-shot shooting scripts. Each line: camera, focus, headline, field note, spoken line.
The numbered chapters are the in-room schedule, not the runtime of the film.
"""
MINUTES = (8, 17, 15, 15, 18, 17)
LABS = ('Baseline', 'Role agents', 'Reusable skills', 'Connectivity', 'Session handoff', 'Browser release')

SESSION = """
starter|0|What did the last developer leave behind?|A conversation is evidence, not yet a handoff.|Imagine inheriting a route migration. You can see three saved conversations in Chronicle, but which change landed, which check passed, and what should you do next?
baseline|1|Start with a known reader|Node 22+ · no package installation|Lab zero gives us eight minutes to establish a baseline. Chronicle already parses two session formats and reconstructs file changes. A passing local suite gives us something to compare against after each change.
roles|0|One request, several responsibilities|The lead plans; a worker edits.|Lab one spends seventeen minutes on ownership. A single public orchestrator reads, searches, delegates and tracks a checklist. It cannot edit the application, so a request has to cross an explicit boundary to the developer.
roles|2|A green test is not a security review|Evidence returns to the lead before sign-off.|The tester can run the code. The security reviewer can only read and search. Documentation explains the contract. If a test fails or the reviewer finds a serious leak, the lead sends the change back rather than declaring victory.
skills|0|Separate a role from its recipe|Agent = who may act. Skill = how to repeat the work.|Lab two gives the team fifteen minutes to encode its methods. The developer's zero-package skill teaches an atomic write: build a temporary file and rename it, so a partial write never becomes the saved configuration.
skills|1|Make failure repeatable|A local stand-in is a test fixture, not a live agent.|The tester's playbook replaces identity and model servers with loopback mocks. Security checks what can leave the process. Documentation specifies the handoff format. Each skill name matches its folder and is attached to the matching role.
config|0|A configured app is not a connected app|Public config reports presence, never the saved secret.|Lab three takes fifteen minutes. The dialog collects the tenant, client, secret, endpoint and agent name. The browser may learn that a secret is saved, but must not retrieve its value from the configuration API.
auth|1|Two exchanges, two kinds of evidence|Entra grants a token. Foundry answers the test.|The local server asks Entra for a token, then uses it for a Foundry Responses request. A READY reply proves more than saving the settings. Separate authentication and agent timings help locate a failure without printing credentials.
finished|0|Choose the evidence deliberately|Only use a session approved for model processing.|Lab four has eighteen minutes to turn a selected session into portable Markdown. The button lives next to Read full session. The input is a read-only source; the new summary belongs in a separate output folder.
map|1|Shrink a long session without losing the ending|Small digest: one final pass. Large digest: map, then synthesize.|Build a chronological digest of prompts, recorded decisions, tools and edits. Replace machine paths with workspace-relative ones. A short digest goes straight to synthesis; a long one becomes bounded chunks whose individual digests feed the final pass.
log|1|Prove the handoff is useful|A log explains execution; a summary explains what to do next.|The progress dialog reports phases, not just a spinner. Inspect a redacted operation log for the job history. Then read the Markdown as the next developer: can you name a tested result and the next unfinished task?
release|0|Ship a browser asset, not an installer|The PNG is served by the same loopback server.|Lab five closes with seventeen minutes on release evidence. A pure Node renderer produces the icon. Test its URL, media type and signature. A visible browser tab icon alone could be an old cached copy.
release|2|What crosses machines?|Take the Markdown. Leave credentials and source sessions behind.|The optional desktop wrapper adds a sandboxed window, a separate server process and narrow native pickers. Inspect it without running it. Our deliverable is still the browser app, and the next developer gets a portable plan instead of somebody else's environment.
"""
INSTRUCTOR = """
starter|0|Stage a story the room can finish|Prepare an approved multi-turn fixture and an empty output folder.|Before learners arrive, open the starter directly in VS Code. Stage a small session with a decision and a follow-up. Ask the room to predict what the next developer would need; return to their answers at the end.
baseline|2|Start the clock with evidence|At 08:00, the baseline should be green.|Use the first eight minutes for three checks: the workspace root, a current Node runtime and a passing suite. Select Agent mode and a frontier model. Distribute the five connection values privately, not in the projected chat.
roles|0|Make delegation visible|Lab 01 · 10 min workers / 5 min lead / 2 min inspection|In the next seventeen minutes, project the customizations view. Ask someone to identify the one role that should appear in the chat picker. Then show why the lead has an agent tool but no edit or execute permission.
roles|2|Rescue a missing worker|Inspect the folder and YAML before rewriting prompts.|If the team does not appear, check which folder VS Code opened and inspect the frontmatter warnings. If a security worker proposes a patch, ask who actually has permission to edit. The reviewer should return a finding with a file and line.
skills|3|Teach the difference with a swap test|Changing a recipe must not grant a tool.|For lab two, reserve ten of fifteen minutes for skill creation, three for attachment and two for discovery. Ask: if we replace the docs playbook, can the writer suddenly run a terminal? No. Permissions belong to the agent.
skills|1|Keep the workshop moving|Before minute 40: four matching names, four attachments, one new chat.|A missing skill usually warrants a spelling check before another generation attempt. Compare the directory, frontmatter name and worker reference side by side. Have everyone switch to Chronicle Orchestrator before dispatching the first feature.
config|0|Separate the private entry from the public demo|Do not project real values; a saved indicator is not a receipt.|In lab three, allow ten minutes for implementation and five for the live test. Project the fixture dialog for orientation, then blank the projector for real entry. Ask learners what the public settings endpoint should reveal about the secret: only whether it exists.
auth|1|Triage by the last completed exchange|Identity failure ≠ missing Foundry permission.|If token acquisition fails, check the tenant, client and secret privately. If the token succeeds but Foundry forbids the call, check the project target and Foundry Agent Consumer assignment. Timebox the detour; label a mock-only result honestly.
finished|0|Rehearse the moment of choice|Lab 04 · 12 min build / 6 min demonstration|In the eighteen-minute handoff lab, have a learner choose a session they are permitted to send. Ask for one known fact and one open task before clicking Create session summary. Those are your acceptance criteria, not the word count.
map|2|Force the interesting branch locally|Use a small chunk limit in a test, not a huge private history.|Demonstrate the multi-chunk path with a deterministic test. The expected calls are a digest for each part and one final synthesis. Ask why a short session skips the extra round trips. Keep the live example small enough to finish.
log|1|When the popup is blocked|Use the inline fallback; share only a reviewed, redacted log.|Show how the log differs from the Markdown output. If the popup is blocked, use the inline fallback. If a job fails, inspect its last phase rather than rerunning blindly. Never ask someone to paste a token or a raw credential file.
release|1|Protect the final seventeen minutes|8 min change / 6 min browser check / 3 min recap|In lab five, let the tester draft checks while the developer renders the icon, but run them after the asset lands. If time slips, cut the desktop tour to one diagram. Do not skip the direct PNG request or the regression suite.
release|2|Ask for three receipts|Local asset, unchanged history, usable next action.|For the closing recap, ask where the desktop server would run, where its credentials would live, and what ships today. The answers are a separate utility process, writable per-user storage outside the installer, and the package-free browser release.
"""
